<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oświęcim</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Auschwitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oświęcim is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> Voivodeship of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the confluence of the Soła and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of a Silesian Piast duchy, sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> in 1457. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the German occupiers, who called it Auschwitz, built near it the largest Nazi concentration and extermination camp, where about 1.1 million people were murdered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oswiecim.jpg" alt="Oświęcim" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aneta Lazurek · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:O%C5%9Bwi%C4%99cim_-_Uk%C5%82ad_urbanistyczny_AL05.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oświęcim lies 33 kilometres south-east of Katowice, near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and the Soła. Its old town is east of the Soła, around the Main Market Square. The name is Slavic, possibly from the owner of a Slavic gord that stood here in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, or from the Polish word oświęcić, 'to sanctify'. Fourteenth-century German-speaking merchants called the town Auswintz, which by the 15th century had become Auschwitz. Both Polish and German names were in official use under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, and the town has been called Oświęcim since 27 January 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Oświęcim was one of the oldest castellan seats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, dating from the 12th century. After the fragmentation of Poland in 1138 it passed to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, and the first Mongol invasion destroyed it in 1241. Around 1272 the rebuilt town received a municipal charter, confirmed on 3 September 1291.</p><p>A separate Duchy of Oświęcim was established around 1315; in 1327 Duke Jan I the Scholastic joined it with the Duchy of Zator, and it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> for over a century.</p><p>In 1454 Duke Jan IV pledged allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and in 1457 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> bought the rights to the town. In 1564 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> integrated the duchies of Oświęcim and Zator into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>.</p><p>The town traded in salt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wieliczka">Wieliczka</a>, and the Swedish invasion burned it in 1655. In 1772 it passed to the Habsburg Empire as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, where it stayed until 1918, and in the second half of the 19th century it became an important rail junction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in the town on the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, fewer than half its people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> annexed the area in October 1939 and expanded former Polish barracks into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auschwitz-concentration-camp">Auschwitz concentration camp</a>. In 1941 a large IG Farben chemical plant was built on the eastern outskirts.</p><p>To keep the area around the camp empty, the Germans planned a 40-square-kilometre buffer zone and expelled the Polish residents in 1940 and 1941; some 17,000 people left their homes, eight villages were wiped off the map, and the town's population shrank to 7,600 by April 1941. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> liberated the camp on 27 January 1945. The Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, preserves the camp site, and the town remains a railway junction and a centre of the chemical industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oświęcim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From May 1943 Croatian Jews were deported by cattle car to <b>Auschwitz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Vel' d'Hiv Roundup of 16 and 17 July 1942, French police under René Bousquet and Jean Leguay arrested 13,152 Jews, including 4,051 children, whom the Gestapo had not asked for, and sent them through Drancy to <b>Auschwitz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was deported to <b>Auschwitz</b> in 1943 and died on a death march in 1945.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marechal-nous-voila">Maréchal, nous voilà !</a></li><li><blockquote>Red Army soldiers reached <b>Auschwitz</b> and found about 7,000 prisoners left behind.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-27">27 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Auschwitz</b> grew into the largest Nazi death camp, where about 1.1 million people, most of them Jews, were murdered.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-20">20 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Oswiecim</b>, west of Krakow, belonged to the Silesian Duchy of Cieszyn.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-oswiecim">Duchy of Oswiecim</a></li></ul></section>
